  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">IN BEST HYDRATE</p>
    <h2>Why sodium bicarbonate earns a place in the formula.</h2>
    <p><strong>First, physiology:</strong> bicarbonate is the principal extracellular buffer in human blood and helps accept hydrogen ions generated during metabolism and hard exercise.</p>
    <p><strong>Second, formulation:</strong> using sodium bicarbonate means the sodium profile does not come from chloride alone. It adds bicarbonate as a second physiologically familiar anion and changes the prepared drink’s acid-base chemistry.</p>
    <p><strong>Third, precedent:</strong> bicarbonate was historically used as the base component in oral rehydration salts; modern World Health Organization formulations use citrate largely because it stores better, not because base chemistry stopped mattering.</p>
    <p><strong>That is the Bicarb Bonus™: sodium plus a real extracellular buffer ion, used here as part of hydration rather than as a loading protocol.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">SAME INGREDIENT, DIFFERENT USE</p>
  <h2>Hydration is not bicarbonate loading.</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article><h3>In the body</h3><p>Bicarbonate is continuously produced, transported and regulated as part of normal physiology.</p></article>
    <article><h3>In hydration</h3><p>Sodium bicarbonate can contribute sodium + bicarbonate as one part of an electrolyte formula.</p></article>
    <article><h3>In loading protocols</h3><p>Sports studies often use much larger acute doses, commonly around 0.2–0.5 g/kg body mass.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">PERFORMANCE RESEARCH</p>
  <h2>Three studies that show why bicarbonate keeps getting attention.</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article><h3>2026 · late-ride power</h3><p>Eleven trained cyclists took carbohydrate with or without bicarbonate during 3 hours of riding. The bicarbonate condition used 18 g sodium bicarbonate + 2 g potassium bicarbonate and produced <strong>12 W higher</strong> mean power in the later 12-minute time trial. Sprint and 2-minute outcomes were not significantly different.</p><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42788859/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the trial →</a></article>
    <article><h3>2024 · 40-km cycling</h3><p>Fourteen trained male cyclists used <strong>0.3 g/kg sodium bicarbonate</strong> in a carbohydrate hydrogel system. The bicarbonate condition improved 40-km time-trial performance by about <strong>54 seconds</strong> versus placebo.</p><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39068627/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the trial →</a></article>
    <article><h3>2021 · position stand</h3><p>The International Society of Sports Nutrition concluded that sodium bicarbonate can improve muscular endurance and high-intensity cycling, running, swimming, rowing and combat-sport performance, with effects most established for efforts lasting roughly <strong>30 seconds to 12 minutes</strong>.</p><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34503527/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the position stand →</a></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">WHY BUFFERING MATTERS</p>
    <h2>The mechanism is bigger than a single performance claim.</h2>
    <p>During high-intensity exercise, hydrogen ions accumulate in and around working muscle. Extracellular bicarbonate helps buffer that acid load; higher-dose sodium bicarbonate protocols can raise blood bicarbonate and strengthen that extracellular buffering environment.</p>
    <p>A 2024 randomized cycling study using <strong>0.2 g/kg</strong> did increase pre-exercise blood bicarbonate, even though it did not show sufficient performance efficacy. That distinction matters: <strong>the buffering physiology can move before a measurable performance outcome does.</strong></p>
    <p>This is a stronger reason to include bicarbonate in formulation science than to imply that every bicarbonate-containing drink is an ergogenic supplement.</p>
    <p><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38421429/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the low-dose trial →</a> · <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34503527/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ISSN position stand →</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">ORAL HYDRATION HISTORY</p>
    <h2>Bicarbonate has a long hydration history.</h2>
    <p>Earlier oral rehydration formulations used sodium bicarbonate. Modern World Health Organization reduced-osmolarity oral rehydration salts use trisodium citrate, in part because citrate is more stable in storage.</p>
    <p>That history is one reason bicarbonate remains relevant to hydration chemistry today.</p>
  </div>
</section>

